1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,160
Good afternoon. Today's lecture is about
how birds find their way during migration,

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:08,152
a topic that has puzzled
scientists for a very

3
00:00:08,176 --> 00:00:11,645
long time, and one where
we're still discovering new details.

4
00:00:11,646 --> 00:00:13,374
Let's start with timing.

5
00:00:13,398 --> 00:00:16,772
Many songbirds migrate
at night, which might seem

6
00:00:17,010 --> 00:00:19,895
surprising given the obvious
risks of flying in darkness.

7
00:00:19,919 --> 00:00:21,972
There's actually a
good reason for this.

8
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:25,368
Flying in the dark helps
birds to avoid predators,

9
00:00:25,392 --> 00:00:28,227
many of which, like
hawks, hunt by sight during

10
00:00:28,228 --> 00:00:31,108
daylight hours and pose
far less threat once the

11
00:00:31,132 --> 00:00:33,967
sun has gone down. Of
course, not all migration

12
00:00:33,968 --> 00:00:36,947
happens at night. In
daylight, many birds use the

13
00:00:36,971 --> 00:00:40,027
sun as a compass,
orienting their flight direction

14
00:00:40,028 --> 00:00:42,146
relative to its
position in the sky.

15
00:00:42,170 --> 00:00:45,627
But the sun moves across the
sky throughout the day, so how

16
00:00:45,628 --> 00:00:48,117
do birds adjust for that?
They compensate for

17
00:00:48,141 --> 00:00:50,872
the sun's movement by
using their internal clock,

18
00:00:51,180 --> 00:00:54,067
essentially recalculating
their heading continuously

19
00:00:54,091 --> 00:00:56,712
as the hours pass. For
birds that fly at night,

20
00:00:56,824 --> 00:00:58,949
a different celestial
queue takes over.

21
00:00:58,973 --> 00:01:02,172
Birds that fly at night can
learn the pattern of the stars,

22
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:05,582
using the night sky much as
sailors once did for navigation.

23
00:01:05,606 --> 00:01:07,607
This isn't something
birds are simply

24
00:01:07,608 --> 00:01:10,225
born knowing in full.
Experiments have shown that

25
00:01:10,249 --> 00:01:12,827
young birds learn to find
the point around which

26
00:01:12,828 --> 00:01:15,258
the night sky rotates,
essentially learning the

27
00:01:15,282 --> 00:01:17,992
position of what we'd call
the pole star by watching

28
00:01:17,993 --> 00:01:21,070
the sky as chicks. Beyond
visual cues, there's a

29
00:01:21,094 --> 00:01:24,120
third major system at
play. Birds can sense the

30
00:01:24,121 --> 00:01:26,724
Earth's magnetic field,
giving them what's often

31
00:01:26,748 --> 00:01:29,472
described as a kind of
internal compass that works

32
00:01:29,473 --> 00:01:32,738
even under cloud cover.
Exactly how this magnetic

33
00:01:32,762 --> 00:01:36,052
sense works is still debated.
One theory suggests

34
00:01:36,189 --> 00:01:38,909
proteins in the bird's
eyes are involved, allowing

35
00:01:38,933 --> 00:01:41,987
birds to actually visualize
magnetic field lines as part

36
00:01:41,988 --> 00:01:45,463
of what they see. An older theory
proposed something different.

37
00:01:45,487 --> 00:01:47,667
The theory about
iron-rich cells in the

38
00:01:47,668 --> 00:01:50,547
beak is now largely doubted,
as more recent research

39
00:01:50,571 --> 00:01:53,192
has failed to support
the idea that beak tissue

40
00:01:53,304 --> 00:01:55,602
plays a central role
in magnetic sensing.

41
00:01:55,626 --> 00:01:59,192
As birds get closer to their
final destination, their navigation

42
00:01:59,193 --> 00:02:02,740
strategy seems to shift.
Near their destination, birds

43
00:02:02,764 --> 00:02:06,060
rely more on visual
landmarks, coastlines, rivers,

44
00:02:06,061 --> 00:02:08,786
and familiar terrain
features to pinpoint the

45
00:02:08,810 --> 00:02:11,907
exact site they're heading
for. Interestingly, some

46
00:02:11,908 --> 00:02:14,864
seabirds may use their
sense of smell over the ocean,

47
00:02:14,888 --> 00:02:17,752
detecting particular scent
cues carried on the wind

48
00:02:17,915 --> 00:02:21,518
to help locate isolated
islands. Now, here's a fascinating

49
00:02:21,542 --> 00:02:25,232
point about how migratory
direction develops in young birds.

50
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:27,361
The direction of
a young bird's first

51
00:02:27,385 --> 00:02:30,467
migration is largely
inherited. It isn't learned from

52
00:02:30,468 --> 00:02:35,340
parents or other birds, but is somehow
encoded genetically. This was demonstrated

53
00:02:35,341 --> 00:02:37,746
dramatically in a
classic experiment.

54
00:02:37,770 --> 00:02:41,260
In the 1950s experiment,
starlings were moved from the

55
00:02:41,261 --> 00:02:43,423
Netherlands to
Switzerland, partway through

56
00:02:43,447 --> 00:02:46,481
their normal migration route
to see how they would respond.

57
00:02:46,482 --> 00:02:48,538
What happened
next was revealing.

58
00:02:48,562 --> 00:02:51,267
The young starlings
continued flying in their

59
00:02:51,268 --> 00:02:53,860
original direction,
simply shifted sideways by

60
00:02:53,884 --> 00:02:56,547
the distance they'd been
displaced, rather than

61
00:02:56,548 --> 00:02:59,629
correcting their course. As
a result, many of the young

62
00:02:59,653 --> 00:03:02,467
birds ended up in Spain
and France, well off their

63
00:03:02,468 --> 00:03:05,305
usual wintering grounds
because they had no way of

64
00:03:05,329 --> 00:03:08,629
recalculating their position.
But here's the key contrast.

65
00:03:08,630 --> 00:03:10,830
Adult birds were
able to correct their

66
00:03:10,854 --> 00:03:13,747
route, adjusting their
flight to still reach their

67
00:03:13,748 --> 00:03:16,949
normal wintering areas
despite the same displacement.

68
00:03:16,973 --> 00:03:20,067
This suggests that experienced
birds seem to have a

69
00:03:20,068 --> 00:03:22,981
mental map, not just a
compass. They know not only

70
00:03:23,005 --> 00:03:26,232
which direction to fly, but
roughly where they actually

71
00:03:26,245 --> 00:03:28,387
are in relation to
their destination.

72
00:03:28,411 --> 00:03:32,152
Much of what we know today comes
from modern tracking technology.

73
00:03:32,190 --> 00:03:34,676
Researchers attach
tiny tracking devices to

74
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:37,432
individual birds, allowing
scientists to follow

75
00:03:37,433 --> 00:03:40,850
entire migratory journeys
in real time. This research

76
00:03:40,874 --> 00:03:43,992
has also revealed some
troubling modern threats.

77
00:03:44,030 --> 00:03:46,200
Artificial light in
cities can disorient

78
00:03:46,224 --> 00:03:48,947
night-migrating birds,
drawing them off course and

79
00:03:48,948 --> 00:03:51,826
sometimes causing fatal
collisions with buildings.

80
00:03:51,850 --> 00:03:54,627
In response, some
cities turn off lights in tall

81
00:03:54,628 --> 00:03:57,095
buildings during peak
migration periods, a simple

82
00:03:57,119 --> 00:03:59,347
measure that has been
shown to significantly

83
00:03:59,348 --> 00:04:02,039
reduce bird deaths. That's
a good place to pause.

84
00:04:02,063 --> 00:04:06,420
Next time we'll look at how climate
change is is affecting migration timing.

